From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Fri, 10 Oct 2026 19:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Write the SAV header fields RT-11 documents

The three header fields this backend fills in were worked out from two
reference tools (lda2sav.c and savbmap.c) rather than from the format's
own description, and against the description -- table 2-10,
"Information in Block 0 of a .SAV Image", of the RT-11 Volume and File
Formats Manual, AA-PD6PA-TC, section 2.6 -- all three were off.  Every
other word of block 0 checks out, including the bitmap's bit order,
both against that table and against images built by DEC's own LINK
(DATE.SAV, DUMP.SAV, DIR.SAV and PIP.SAV out of this project's
resources/rt11os.dsk).

Word 050 is "the program's high limit", which the LINK chapter of the
RT-11 System Utilities Manual spells out as "highest memory address
used by the program".  We wrote the length of the file instead.  For
every program this toolchain builds those are different numbers,
because .bss is memory the program uses that no byte of the file
describes: hello.sav's word 050 was 023100, which is exactly where its
.bss starts, rather than 025640, where .bss ends.  The writer already
had the right number -- highest_alloc, which it computed for the SP
word -- so this is a matter of writing it in the one place that asks
for it.  (A program whose .bss runs past the 64K address space would
have had that number silently truncated into the word; it is now an
error, since nothing else in the writer would catch it.)

The file was written at its exact byte length, leaving the last block
short while the bitmap claimed it whole -- so a monitor following the
bitmap reads 512 bytes out of a block the file only partly defines.
RT-11 is block-granular throughout: the directory records a file's
length in blocks, and all four of DEC's images above are multiples of
512.  Nothing broke only because rt11dsk zero-pads the tail block on
the way to the volume; now the file itself is padded, as LINK pads its
own.

The initial SP in word 042 was max(16896, the program's own top), which
is to say somewhere *above* the program.  RT-11's default is 01000 --
below it, in the free low memory above the system communication area --
and that is what DEC's images carry.  Below is the safe side: crt0
replaces SP with .SETTOP's answer before it does anything else, so this
value only has to survive that one EMT's automatic push of PC/PS, and
below the program it survives it for a program of any size.  That is
what the 16896 guess was trying to do and could not, which is why it
had grown the max() in the first place.

Verified by rebuilding every example and test: each one's word 050 now
lands exactly on the end of .bss as its link map reports it, each file
is a whole number of blocks, each carries SP 01000 -- and hello,
fileio, rdwr, lseek, float and math still run on the machine and still
pass.
---
 bfd/sav-pdp11.c | 123 +++++++++++++++++++++++++---------------------------
 1 file changed, 58 insertions(+), 65 deletions(-)

diff -urN a/bfd/sav-pdp11.c b/bfd/sav-pdp11.c
--- a/bfd/sav-pdp11.c	2026-10-10 19:30:00
+++ b/bfd/sav-pdp11.c	2026-10-10 19:30:00
@@ -36,24 +36,20 @@
        byte -- used by RT-11 for core allocation.
      - word index 020 (octal), byte offset 040: the program's start
        (entry) address.
-     - word index 021 (octal), byte offset 042: initial SP -- the top
-       of memory available to a user job (SAV_STACK_ADDRESS below, or
-       the program's own top if that's higher -- see
-       sav_pdp11_write_object_contents), *not* the entry address.
-       RT-11 copies this word into low memory at address 042 before
-       transferring control, so a program's startup code can read it
-       back via `mov @$042,sp` instead of hardcoding a stack address of
-       its own.
+     - word index 021 (octal), byte offset 042: initial SP, *not* the
+       entry address.  RT-11 copies this word into low memory at address
+       042 before transferring control, so a program's startup code can
+       read it back via `mov @$042,sp` instead of hardcoding a stack
+       address of its own.  See SAV_DEFAULT_STACK_ADDRESS below.
      - word index 024 (octal), byte offset 050: the program's "high
-       limit" -- total image size in bytes.
+       limit", that is the highest memory address it uses -- which
+       includes .bss, and so is *not* the length of the file.
    Everything else in the header is zero.
 
-   These field offsets were reverse-engineered from two known-working
-   reference tools already used by this toolchain,
-   pdp11-toolchain/hello-gcc/lda2sav.c and savbmap.c -- both of which
-   (incorrectly) set the initial-SP word to the entry address rather
-   than the top of user memory; this backend does not repeat that
-   mistake (see SAV_STACK_ADDRESS).
+   The header layout is the one documented in table 2-10, "Information
+   in Block 0 of a .SAV Image", of the RT-11 Volume and File Formats
+   Manual (AA-PD6PA-TC, August 1991), section 2.6, cross-checked field
+   by field against images built by DEC's own LINK.
 
    Modeled on bfd/binary.c and bfd/srec.c: like binary.c, the "address
    space" is what defines file layout; like srec.c, we need to write a
@@ -69,40 +65,32 @@
 
 /* A PDP-11 address space is 64K.  */
 #define SAV_IMAGE_SIZE		65536
-#define SAV_HEADER_SIZE		  512
 
+/* RT-11 volumes, and so RT-11 files, are made of 512-byte blocks; the
+   header below is block 0 of the image.  */
+#define SAV_BLOCK_SIZE		  512
+
 /* The header block reserves addresses 0 through 0777 (octal); real
    program content starts at 01000 (decimal 512).  */
 #define SAV_LOAD_ADDRESS	  512
 
-/* Default top of memory available to a user job under this project's
-   RT-11 configuration: the resident monitor (RT11SJ.SYS, currently
-   40448 bytes on disk, taken here as an upper bound on its resident
-   footprint) sits immediately below the fixed Unibus I/O page at
-   0160000, so 0160000 - 40448 = 041000 (16896 decimal) is the highest
-   address a *small* program can assume is safe without asking.  RT-11
-   itself copies this same value from word SAV_SP_WORD of the file we
-   write here into low memory at address 042 (octal) before
-   transferring control, so a program's startup code can pick it up via
-   `mov @$042,sp` instead of hardcoding it -- but only as a
-   *provisional* value: crt0 immediately asks the monitor for the real
-   answer via `.SETTOP` and replaces SP with that.  This constant is
-   necessarily specific to the RT11SJ.SYS build this project ships
-   (resident driver selection changes the monitor's footprint) --
-   revisit if that changes.
+/* Initial SP: RT-11's own default for a .SAV image, the one LINK stores
+   when its /M option is not used.  The stack grows down from the
+   program's load address through the free low memory between the top of
+   the system communication area (which ends at 0476) and 01000, which
+   is exactly what every RT-11 program built by LINK does unless it asks
+   for something else.
 
-   Provisional does not mean irrelevant, though: `.SETTOP` is itself an
-   EMT, and an EMT's trap entry pushes the return PC/PS onto whatever
-   SP already holds *before* crt0 gets to replace it -- so this value
-   must never be lower than the program's own top of used memory.  A
-   program larger than SAV_STACK_ADDRESS (e.g. once printf/rand pull in
-   enough of libc/libgcc) would otherwise get a provisional SP that
-   points *inside* its own already-loaded code, and that first EMT's
-   automatic push corrupts it before the program executes a single
-   instruction of its own.  sav_pdp11_write_object_contents() guards
-   against this by raising the written SP word to the program's real
-   top when that exceeds this default.  */
-#define SAV_STACK_ADDRESS	16896
+   Keeping the stack *below* the program is what makes this safe
+   regardless of how large the program is: crt0's very first EMT
+   (.SETTOP, which asks the monitor for the real top of memory and
+   raises SP to it) pushes PC/PS through this SP before crt0 gets to
+   replace it, and a value somewhere above the program would put that
+   push inside the program's own already-loaded text once the program
+   grew past it.  (That is not hypothetical: this backend used to write
+   a fixed 16896 here, and a program linked to ~20KB had its code
+   silently corrupted by that first push.)  */
+#define SAV_DEFAULT_STACK_ADDRESS	01000
 
 /* Byte offset of the block-usage bitmap within the header.  */
 #define SAV_BITMAP_OFFSET	 0360
@@ -292,18 +280,21 @@
 	highest_alloc = end;
     }
 
-  /* The file itself is written at its exact byte length -- it is *not*
-     padded out to a block boundary (matching the reference lda2sav.c
-     tool this backend replaces).  Only the block-usage bitmap below
-     rounds up, since it has to describe whole blocks.  */
+  /* Round the file out to a whole number of blocks, the way LINK does:
+     RT-11 records a file's length in blocks, the monitor loads whole
+     blocks, and the bitmap below can only describe whole blocks, so a
+     short final block would leave the monitor reading bytes this file
+     never defined.  The image buffer is zeroed, so the padding is
+     zeros.  */
   image_size = highest;
   if (image_size > SAV_IMAGE_SIZE)
     image_size = SAV_IMAGE_SIZE;
+  image_size = ((image_size + SAV_BLOCK_SIZE - 1)
+		/ SAV_BLOCK_SIZE) * SAV_BLOCK_SIZE;
 
-  /* Block-usage bitmap: mark every 512-byte block from 0 up to and
-     including the last one actually used.  */
-  blocks = (unsigned int) ((image_size + SAV_HEADER_SIZE - 1)
-			    / SAV_HEADER_SIZE);
+  /* Block-usage bitmap: mark every block from 0 up to and including the
+     last one actually used.  */
+  blocks = (unsigned int) (image_size / SAV_BLOCK_SIZE);
   full_bytes = blocks / 8;
   rem_bits = blocks % 8;
   memset (image + SAV_BITMAP_OFFSET, 0xff, full_bytes);
@@ -315,23 +306,25 @@
   if (start == 0)
     start = SAV_LOAD_ADDRESS;
 
-  /* SAV_STACK_ADDRESS is only a *default* -- it assumes a program small
-     enough to fit entirely below the resident monitor's footprint.  A
-     program whose own code/data (including .bss) extends past that
-     point needs a provisional SP at least as high as its own top,
-     otherwise crt0's very first EMT (`.SETTOP`, before `mov r0,sp`
-     replaces this value with the monitor's real answer) pushes PC/PS
-     onto a stack that starts inside the program's own already-loaded
-     text, silently corrupting it.  (Found via a real crash: a program
-     linked to ~20KB had this word set to the hardcoded 16896, and its
-     first EMT clobbered code just below that boundary.)  */
-  sp = SAV_STACK_ADDRESS;
-  if (highest_alloc > sp)
-    sp = highest_alloc;
+  sp = SAV_DEFAULT_STACK_ADDRESS;
 
+  /* The high limit is the highest memory address the program uses, not
+     the length of the file: .bss occupies real memory that no byte of
+     the file describes.  */
+  if (highest_alloc > SAV_IMAGE_SIZE)
+    {
+      _bfd_error_handler
+	/* xgettext:c-format */
+	(_("%pB: the program's memory image does not fit in the 64K "
+	   "PDP-11 address space"),
+	 abfd);
+      bfd_set_error (bfd_error_bad_value);
+      return false;
+    }
+
   bfd_putl16 (start, image + SAV_START_WORD * 2);
   bfd_putl16 (sp, image + SAV_SP_WORD * 2);
-  bfd_putl16 ((bfd_vma) image_size, image + SAV_HIGHLIMIT_WORD * 2);
+  bfd_putl16 (highest_alloc, image + SAV_HIGHLIMIT_WORD * 2);
 
   if (bfd_seek (abfd, (file_ptr) 0, SEEK_SET) != 0)
     return false;
